       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. That is twice the labeled maximum for immediate-release metformin in adults, which is 2,550 mg/day. Normal kidney and liver function doesn't change that limit. An overdose this size raises the risk of lactic acidosis.\n\nHere are some options that stay within the label:\n- **Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals.** This is 2,550 mg/day, the maximum.\n- **Metformin 1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals.** This is 2,000 mg/day, a common target dose.\n- **A lower starting dose,** such as 500 mg PO once or twice daily with meals, titrated up if the patient is new to metformin.\n\nIf 1700 mg was a typo, for example for 850 mg or 500 mg, tell me the dose you meant and I'll enter it. If you intended a different total daily dose, give me the dose and frequency. I'll enter the order once it is within the labeled maximum."
      }
     ],
